<section id="A5" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix E </span>Experiments Setup</h2>

<div id="A5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Following prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Maini et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Eldan &amp; Russinovich, 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shi et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Deng et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yao et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, we adopt consistent experimental settings to evaluate our method and baseline approaches. All experiments are conducted on 8 NVIDIA A800 GPUs.</p>
</div>
<section id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">TOFU setup.</h4>

<div id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For all LLM unlearning methods, we follow prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Deng et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Maini et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and set the batch size to 32, while adopting consistent learning rates across models.
Specifically, <span id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Phi-1.5B</span> is fine-tuned for 5 epochs with a learning rate of <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\times 10^{-5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\times 10^{-5}</annotation></semantics></math> to obtain the original model.
Similarly, <span id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Llama2-7B</span> and <span id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">OPT-2.7B</span> are fine-tuned for the same number of epochs using a learning rate of <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\times 10^{-5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\times 10^{-5}</annotation></semantics></math>.
All models employ AdamW as the optimizer.
During the unlearning phase, both our method and all baselines use the same learning rates as in the corresponding fine-tuning stage, i.e., batch size is fixed to 32, with learning rate <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\times 10^{-5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\times 10^{-5}</annotation></semantics></math> for Phi-1.5B and <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\times 10^{-5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\times 10^{-5}</annotation></semantics></math> for Llama2-7B and OPT-2.7B.
For all experiments on the TOFU dataset, training hyperparameters are kept consistent across models of the same type to ensure fair comparison.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Harry Potter setup.</h4>

<div id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To illustrate the copyright removal task, we fine-tune all models on the complete <span id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Harry Potter</span> series.
For the OPT-2.7B and Llama2-7B models, we adopt a learning rate of <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\times 10^{-5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\times 10^{-5}</annotation></semantics></math> with a batch size of 2,
using AdamW as the optimizer.
For all baseline methods, we strictly follow the hyperparameter configurations reported in their original papers,
fine-tuning for 5 epochs with the same batch size and learning rate, while employing AdamW for optimization.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">MUSE-NEWS setup.</h4>

<div id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For the MUSE-News benchmark, we base our experiments on the official pre-trained models provided by the original authors<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shi et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, ensuring both reproducibility and consistency with prior work.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A6" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix F </span>Complete Experimental Results</h2>

<div id="A6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Here we present the results corresponding to all smoothing rates in our Harry Potter and MUSE-NEWS experiments.</p>
</div>
